      "type": "text",
      "text": "I ran a standard RSI strategy on CRCL (Circle) from its June 5, 2025 IPO to today. It lost money. It turned $10,000 into about $5,411, a **\u221245.9%** return, and never got back to its starting value.\n\n**Setup:** 14-day RSI. It buys when RSI drops below 30 and sells when it rises above 70. It only buys (no short selling), puts all $10k into each trade, and has no stop-loss.\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Total return | \u221245.9% (\u221249.4% annualized) |\n| Max drawdown | \u221262.0% (lowest point Feb 5, 2026) |\n| Sharpe / Sortino | \u22120.29 / \u22120.45 |\n| Trades | 3 (1 win, 2 losses) |\n| Profit factor | 0.17 |\n| Avg win / avg loss | +$921 / \u2212$2,755 |\n\n**Trades:**\n1. **Aug 5 \u2192 Dec 10, 2025:** bought at $154.08, sold at $88.32, **\u221242.8% (\u2212$4,279)**. This one trade caused most of the loss.\n2. **Jan 23 \u2192 Feb 25, 2026:** bought at $71.40, sold at $83.06, **+16.1% (+$921)**.\n3. **Apr 6 \u2192 Aug 13, 2026:** bought at $92.24, sold at $75.30, **\u221218.5% (\u2212$1,230)**.\n\nThe strategy has held no shares since Aug 13.\n\n**Why it failed:**\n- **Buying the dip didn't work in a steady decline.** CRCL shot up after the IPO and then fell for months. The strategy bought when RSI said \"oversold\" in early August, but the stock kept falling. The only way out was RSI rising above 70, so it held from about $154 down to $88, and at the worst point the account was down about 59%.\n- **No stop-loss.** With all the money in and no exit rule for losses, one bad trade wiped out nearly half the account.\n- **Three trades prove very little.** This isn't a real test of RSI on CRCL. It mostly reflects how badly the first entry was timed. The first RSI reading only came about two weeks after the IPO, and the first buy signal came two months in.\n\n**What I could run next:**\n- A buy-and-hold comparison over the same dates, to see whether RSI did better or worse than just holding.\n- A parameter search on RSI (period and buy/sell levels), plus a walk-forward test to check that the result isn't just fitted to this one period.\n- A side-by-side test of other strategies (for example momentum or moving-average crossover) on CRCL, since this stock has so far behaved more like a trending stock than one that bounces back after drops."
